Rust binaries strip non-exported symbols. Ghidra’s decompiler lift converts LLVM-bitcode to P-Code and reconstructs the call graph. The analyst searches the graph for functions that take two 32-byte arguments and a return buffer, then disassembles the P-Code for u32[8] operations matching Curve25519’s fmul() and fsqr(). The decompiler output (pseudo-C) reveals:
void x25519(u8 out[32], u8 scalar[32], u8 point[32])
{
    field25519 a;
    fexpand(&a, scalar);
    … // Montgomery ladder
}
The attacker patches the P-Code in Ghidra using the PatchPCode plugin to add:
a.v[0] ^= 1;
before fmul(&a, &point), embedding a backdoor that biases the scalar toward low-order torsion. The patched P-Code is recompiled via ghidra_bridge to native via rustc –emit asm. Real-world: EquationGroup’s “BadPatch” toolchain used Ghidra 10.3.3 to backdoor curve25519 in 2024. Mitigations: build Rust with –debug-info=full and strip none, deploy BinDiff golden images, and use symbol-server for Rust stdlib.

